ytes (32 ints) maybe. But careful: when j near end, prefetch beyond array may fault? __builtin_prefetch on invalid address is safe? It's a hint and should not fault; but to be safe only if j + PREFETCH_DIST < n.\n\nWe'll implement:\nconst int PF = 64;\nif (j + PF < n) __builtin_prefetch(oldRow + j + PF, 0, 1);\ninside unrolled loop maybe every iteration? That's many prefetches; but they are cheap. Could do once per 4 iterations.\n\nLet's keep simple: no prefetch to avoid risk. The role A includes memory locality; alignment and sequential access already good.\n\nBut we need propose one candidate relevant to \"memory locality, loop order, data layout and blocking\". Our transformations:\n- Cache-line aligned allocations for col and ring (data layout).\n- Unrolled inner loops by 4 to improve instruction-level parallelism and reduce branch overhead (loop order/blocking? unrolling is a form of blocking at instruction level).\n- Fused initial hsum computation with column update? We didn't fuse; we kept separate. Could mention that we keep separate for vectorization.\n\nMaybe add `restrict` qualifiers to help compiler. Let's include:\nlong long* __restrict__ c = col;\nint* __restrict__ r = ring;\nThen use c and r in loops. But oldRow points into r; if we declare r restrict, then any pointer derived from r also restricted? In C++, `__restrict__` on a pointer means accesses through that pointer don't alias other pointers not based on it. Since col is separate allocation, fine.\n\nLet's modify code to use c and r:\nlong long* __restrict__ c = col;\nint* __restrict__ rr = ring;\nThen in loops use c[j], rr[...]. But oldRow = rr + head*n; that pointer derived from restrict rr, so compiler knows it doesn't alias c. Good.\n\nLet's write final code carefully with includes and free.\n\nAlso ensure FastScanner remains same.\n\nPotential issue: posix_memalign requires <cstdlib> and may need _POSIX_C_SOURCE defined? On Linux with g++ default, should be available. Could use `aligned_all